Hi,

the factor of two has to do with the number of parameters
estimated/stored, i.e. storing theta, vs (thetaA, thetaB).  I cannot
reproduce this, but I one guess is that you have tried both
combineAlleles=TRUE and combineAlleles=FALSE and somehow there exist
cached indices that are reused from one model setting to the other.
This should not happen, but this is my best guess.  Did you really do
those lines of code, or did you try do something like?

 plm$combineAlleles <- newStatus

If you did, I discovered a potential bug in that code, but I still
didn't manage to reproduce what you got.  Please let me know exactly
what you tried, because that helps us find the cause of this.

If you instead change the settings using:

plm <- RmaCnPlm(csN, mergeStrands=TRUE, combineAlleles=FALSE)

and then do, say:

plm <- RmaCnPlm(csN, mergeStrands=FALSE, combineAlleles=FALSE)

it should work.  When worse come to worse, you can always restart R
and rerun your script.

FYI, this is a good example why there are a lot of internal validation
tests that asserts that intermediate results are what they are
expected to be.  We never expect that our code is bug free and we
therefore code as there are bugs and try our best to catch the early
on.  (I'm not sure if this is really a bug or if it occurred for using
a unexpected "backdoor").

BTW, the word "crash" should be reserved for real crashes, i.e. core
dumps and sudden death of your R session.  What happens here is an
error/exception that is caught and reported.  When these are caught,
there should be no side effects.  With crashed you do not know what
side effects there are.

Cheers

Henrik

